# <%.Name%>

A gofastr-native app with Go-defined screens. The bundled Android and UIKit
renderers draw them; there is no Swift, Java, Xcode project, or Gradle build.

- `screens/` holds the screens and a small item store.
- `<%.Package%>/` is the gomobile bind package the native host calls.

Module: `<%.Module%>`. <%.NativeNote%>

## Commands

Run these from this directory. `go tool gofastr-native` uses the CLI version
pinned in `go.mod`, so the CLI always matches the library.

```sh
go test ./...
go tool gofastr-native doctor --platform android-emu
go tool gofastr-native run --platform android-emu --renderer native --pkg ./<%.Package%><%.IdentityFlags%>
go tool gofastr-native dev --platform android-emu --renderer native --pkg ./<%.Package%><%.IdentityFlags%>
go tool gofastr-native dev --platform go --pkg ./<%.Package%> --test
```

On a Mac with Xcode, use `--platform ios-sim` instead of `android-emu`.

## What dev does

`dev` watches the Go packages the app imports. After a change it rebuilds,
reinstalls, and restarts the app. It is not hot reload:

- Navigation starts again at the Items list.
- Saved items persist. They live in the app's data directory, which a
  reinstall keeps.
- Before reinstalling, `dev` sends the app to the background so the renderer
  checkpoints open editors, then waits briefly. This is best effort. `dev`
  cannot confirm the checkpoint finished, so edits newer than the last
  completed checkpoint can be lost. `--no-checkpoint` skips this step.
- A failed build leaves the installed app alone.

`--platform go` runs `go vet` and `go build` (and with `--test`, `go test ./...`)
on every change, without a device.

## Storage limits

The starter store keeps up to 63 items, one private file each. Replace it with
your own storage when you outgrow it. A save from two editors of the same item
keeps the last one.
